DATA = "`dataDir/byok/secrets/<validated-id>.bin`; profile metadata stays in `profiles.json`, with no secret. The Windows account is the security principal; DPAPI decryption is scoped to that account. `profileId` comes from API/user metadata: enforce the existing restrictive `PROFILE_ID_PATTERN` before filesystem paths; filenames must remain beneath the configured secrets folder. PowerShell command is constant, not derived from input, and its input/output are bounded. On read, encrypted blob flows into DPAPI and plaintext back through private process stdout into the runtime's existing in-memory BYOK provider, never to logs. Existing user-controlled provider URL remains constrained by `normalizeProfileInput`; no new network requests. Access control is the existing `requireLocalDaemonRequest`; do not replace it. Unverified: Windows ACL inheritance and installed PowerShell availability, runtime restart and real user logon.\n\n## 4. Security invariants\n* No successful `set` until DPAPI encryption succeeds and ciphertext is atomically committed (unique temporary file, rename; cleanup on errors). No plaintext secret written to disk, passed on argv, or put in errors/logs.\n* `get` must reject corrupt/oversized ciphertext and DPAPI failures instead of behaving as a missing key; missing file alone returns null. Encrypt/decrypt bind DPAPI entropy to service and profile ID, preventing profile substitution. Reject invalid profile IDs at the backend boundary, not only service calls.\n* `available` checks native Windows platform and executable probe, not fake availability on Linux test hosts. Backend exceptions are generic and never echo command stderr, output, or secrets. Bound subprocess lifetime and output; kill on timeout/overflow, handle `error` and `close` only once.\n* Deletion removes the encrypted blob; metadata and rollback stay governed by `ByokCredentialService` mutation queue. Never silently fall back to an unencrypted backend or cloud runtime. Preserve legacy Windows cleanup pat"
